    "content": "# Public Research Publication Manifest — 2026-10-03\n\n## Counts currently established by the archive\n\n- **52** bibliography/hunting-list entries.\n- **45** structured source-register records.\n- **12** controlled claims in the claim register.\n- **12** publication-ready research records prepared from those controlled claims.\n\nThese numbers are different layers. They must not be presented as “52 claims verified” or “54/54 cross-checked.”\n\n## Publication status\n\nThe 12 prepared records are marked `published` in the bundled publication seed and are used as a public fallback by the Research page when Firestore has no published records. Firestore remains the authoritative CMS when populated.\n\nThe Admin Editorial Console also contains a one-time **Publish prepared research (12)** action for the owner. It writes the same records to the `research_records` collection and keeps cross-check/evidence/researcher working notes in the separate `research_admin_notes` collection.\n\n## Rights rule\n\nRestricted/copyrighted third-party evidence is represented by citation, catalogue reference, exact target where known, rights status and official repository link. Full restricted scans are not bundled as public copies. The Hamilton evidence extract is retained because the current Library of Congress rights note permits reuse subject to its stated conditions.\n\n## Current public research set\n\n1. Kirat/Kichak communities in eastern Nepal in the early nineteenth century.\n2. Agam Singha in Hamilton's account.\n3. Yellung / Ya'lamba / Yalambar in Kirata dynasty traditions.\n4. Yalambar and the Mahabharata character identification.\n5. Yalambar and the title “Hang”.\n6. Catalogue-dated Kiranta manuscript objects in British Library EAP collections.\n7. Pallo Kirāta historical documents in Documenta Nepalica.\n8. VS 1865 appeal to Kirātīs and Lepchas across the Ṭistā.\n9. E_3420_0013: Pallo Kirāta / Limbuvān administration in VS 1876.\n10. EAP1023/22/3: Kirata Dastabej.\n11. Mundhum as an oral religious and narrative tradition in modern scholarship.\n12. Kirantbamshi Kings and Yalung/Yalambar in Nepal Parichaya.\n\n## Important limitation\n\nThis is the current controlled publication layer, not a claim that all world literature on Kirat history has been exhausted. The archive is designed for continuous addition, cross-checking and versioned publication.\n"
